<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Scullin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Scullin was the Labor prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1929 to 1932, taking office just before the Wall Street Crash. His government struggled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, falling export prices and heavy debts, and the Labor Party split over the response. He appointed Isaac Isaacs as the first Australian-born governor-general and lost office at the election of December 1931.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-scullin.jpg" alt="James Scullin" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of Australia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_James_H._Scullin_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Henry Scullin was born at Trawalla, Victoria, on 18 September 1876, the fourth of eight children of Irish Catholic parents from County Londonderry. His father was a railway labourer. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballarat">Ballarat</a> in 1887, and after leaving school at 12 he did manual odd jobs until about 1900, then ran a grocer's shop for ten years. He read widely, belonged to the Catholic Young Men's Society and was a skilled debater. He remained a devout Catholic, a non-drinker and a non-smoker.</p><p>He was a foundation member of his local Political Labor Council in 1903 and an organiser for the Australian Workers' Union. He won Corangamite in 1910, lost it in 1913, and returned to the House of Representatives in 1922 for Yarra. In March 1928 he replaced Matthew Charlton as Labor leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Scullin came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> with the largest majority Labor had won to that time. His cabinet was sworn in on 22 October 1929, and two days later news of Black Thursday on Wall Street reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Unemployment was just over 13 per cent at the end of 1929, and prices for wool and wheat, the chief exports, had fallen by almost a third. With his treasurer Ted Theodore he proposed plans for debt repayment, relief for farmers and stimulus by deficit spending, but the Senate and the Commonwealth Bank repeatedly blocked them. Theodore resigned in June 1930 after a Royal Commission criticised him over the Mungana affair.</p><p>Scullin's party split. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jack-lang">Jack Lang</a>, Premier of New South Wales, urged repudiation of interest on debts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and when Scullin reappointed Theodore as treasurer in January 1931, Joseph Lyons and James Fenton resigned from the cabinet. In May, with unemployment at 27.6 per cent, Scullin agreed with the premiers to the Premiers' Plan, which cut spending by 20 per cent, including social welfare. Core Labor supporters felt betrayed, and the government was defeated at the 1931 election by the newly formed United Australia Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Scullin remained Labor leader for four more years and lost the 1934 election. He then became a respected authority on public finance and taxation, advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-curtin">John Curtin</a> closely and remained member for Yarra until 1949. Disappointed with his own term, he lived to see the Curtin and Chifley governments carry out many of his ideas, and died in January 1953 at the age of 76.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Scullin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trawalla, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-37.43,143.47&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Trawalla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8137 3511 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Scullin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8587 3781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8587 3781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-37.43,143.47&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
